xv6 is a re-implementation of Dennis Ritchie's and Ken Thompson's Unix
Version 6 (v6).  xv6 loosely follows the structure and style of v6,
but is implemented for a modern RISC-V multiprocessor using ANSI C.

ADDED MODULES

This kernel is extended with 10 new modules. Nine are written in C and
one (Module 9) is written in Rust.

Module 1: Multitasking and Process Information
  - Many processes run at the same time using timer-based preemption.
  - "ps" system call lists every process with its pid, state, name and
    memory size.
  - "getppid" system call returns the parent process id.

Module 2: CPU Scheduling
  - Round-robin scheduling (default).
  - Priority scheduling: "setpriority" system call changes a process
    priority.
  - Lottery scheduling: "settickets" system call gives a process more
    or fewer tickets.

Module 3: System Call Extensions
  - "trace" system call prints the system calls made by a process.
  - "sysinfo" system call reports free memory and number of running
    processes.

Module 4: Lazy Page Allocation
  - Heap memory (sbrk) is allocated only when a page is first used.
  - The page-fault handler allocates the missing page on demand.

Module 5: Synchronization
  - Counting semaphores: sem_open, sem_wait, sem_post.
  - Waiting processes sleep instead of wasting CPU time.
  - Producer-consumer example program.

Module 6: Inter-Process Communication
  - Message queues: mq_send and mq_recv.
  - A process waits (blocks) when the queue is empty or full.

Module 7: Large Files
  - Double-indirect blocks in the file system.
  - Maximum file size grows from about 268 KB to about 64 MB.

Module 8: Symbolic Links
  - "symlink" system call creates a link to another file.
  - Links are followed automatically when a file is opened.
  - Link loops are detected and rejected.

Module 9: Kernel Log Buffer (written in Rust)
  - A no_std Rust library linked into the C kernel.
  - Stores the latest kernel messages in a fixed-size ring buffer.
  - "dmesg" system call and command print the saved kernel messages.

Module 10: User Utilities
  - New commands: ps, free, sleep, uptime.
  - "demo" program that runs a short test of the modules.

ACKNOWLEDGMENTS

xv6 is inspired by John Lions's Commentary on UNIX 6th Edition (Peer
to Peer Communications; ISBN: 1-57398-013-7; 1st edition (June 14,
2000)).  See also https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/, which provides
pointers to on-line resources for v6.

